## Passing Values Transfers Ownership

When you pass a value to a function that takes an owned parameter, ownership moves:

```xiom
fn consume(v: Vec[Int]) {
  io.println(v.len());
  // v is dropped here when function ends
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let my_vec = Vec[Int].new();
  my_vec.push(1);
  my_vec.push(2);
  consume(my_vec);       // Ownership moves into consume
  // io.println(my_vec.len());  // ERROR: my_vec was moved!
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Functions Take Ownership

```xiom
fn process_and_discard(data: Vec[Int]) {
  // Do heavy work with data...
  // No need to free — compiler does it at end of scope
}
```

This pattern is essential for writing functions that take responsibility for cleaning up resources. The function that OWNS the value decides when to free it.

### Ownership Is a Contract

The function signature `fn consume(v: Vec[Int])` says:
- "I need this Vec, and I'm taking full responsibility for it"
- "After you call me, you can't use it anymore"
- "I'll clean it up when I'm done"

This is checked at compile time. No runtime overhead.

